    "passage": "Kamakura is located in Kanagawa Prefecture and used to be the capital of Japan. It is a city steeped in history. Minamoto no Yoritomo [[1]] a military government here at the end of the 12th century. After the collapse of the government, the city fell out of favor temporarily until the Edo Period. In this era, Kamakura regained popularity and was [[2]] a draw for tourists. Despite the ups and downs over the past centuries, the city remains one of the most [[3]] visited destinations in the Kanto region of Japan.\nKamakura [[4]] its historic sites. Two major landmarks are Tsurugaoka Hachimangu and the Great Buddha of Kamakura. The former is a Shinto shrine that serves as a cultural center of Kamakura. A number of important festivals take place in this temple. A large red-colored torii at the entrance is [[5]] of the shrine. As for the latter, it is a huge bronze statue that sits solemnly in a Buddhist temple called Kotoku-in. The Great Buddha, which measures 13.35 meters in height and weighs approximately 121 tons, is a national treasure, a cultural icon, and a source of literary [[6]].\nAside from its historical significance, Kamakura also [[7]] visitors with its scenic spots, especially seaside views. When visitors take the cute green Edoden train along the coast, they can feast their eyes on the [[8]] Pacific Ocean. On the railway line is Enoshima, a popular tourist attraction. It is a small offshore island renowned as the hub of a local [[9]] area. At the vantage point of the island, [[10]] the Enoshima Sea Candle, visitors can have a panoramic view of the entire coastal region. They may even catch a glimpse of Mount Fuji. Better yet, if the weather is clear, adventure seekers can go spelunking in Iwaya Caves.",
